        "### Test Set Generation Analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "**a. Why is it important to have a test set?**\n",
        "\n",
        "It is crucial to have a test set to evaluate the final model's performance on unseen data. This helps in estimating how well the model will generalize to new, real-world examples. If we evaluate the model on the same data it was trained on, it might overfit and show overly optimistic performance metrics that do not reflect its true generalization ability.\n",
        "\n",
        "**b. What is the purpose of setting `random_state`?**\n",
        "\n",
        "Setting `random_state` (or `seed`) ensures reproducibility of the random operations. When splitting datasets (like `train_test_split` or `StratifiedShuffleSplit`) or initializing algorithms that involve randomness, specifying a `random_state` means that the same random sequence will be generated every time the code is run. This is essential for debugging, sharing results, and ensuring that experiments are repeatable.\n",
        "\n",
        "**c. Explain how stratified sampling works and why it is beneficial in this context compared to purely random sampling.**\n",
        "\n",
        "Stratified sampling is a technique where the population is divided into homogeneous subgroups (strata) based on a certain characteristic, and then samples are drawn from each stratum in proportion to their size in the population. In this context, the 'income_cat' (income category) serves as the stratification characteristic. The dataset is split such that each income category is adequately represented in both the training and test sets, maintaining the same proportions as in the overall dataset.\n",
        "\n",
        "It is beneficial compared to purely random sampling because purely random sampling might, by chance, result in a test set (or training set) that is not representative of the overall population, especially if the dataset is not very large or if certain categories are rare. For example, if there were very few districts in the 'ISLAND' `ocean_proximity` category, a purely random split might put all of them in the training set, leaving none for the test set, or vice versa. By ensuring that key features (like income category, which is crucial for predicting housing prices) are proportionally represented, stratified sampling reduces sampling bias and leads to a more reliable evaluation of the model.\n",
        "\n",
        "**d. Comment on the proportions obtained in step 7 and verify that stratified sampling indeed worked as expected.**\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's compare the income category proportions:\n",
