        "id": "4678190b"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Data Preparation Pipeline Analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "**a. How the `SimpleImputer` handles missing values in numerical attributes:**\n",
        "\n",
        "The `SimpleImputer` is used to fill in missing values (NaNs) in numerical attributes. In this pipeline, it was configured with `strategy=\"median\"`. This means that for each numerical column with missing values, the imputer calculates the median of the non-missing values in that specific column during the `fit()` phase. Then, during the `transform()` phase, it replaces all occurrences of missing values in that column with the pre-calculated median. This strategy is robust to outliers compared to using the mean.\n",
        "\n",
        "**b. Number of new columns created by `OneHotEncoder` for the `ocean_proximity` categorical feature:**\n",
        "\n",
        "The `ocean_proximity` categorical feature has 5 unique categories, as seen in the initial data inspection (`housing['ocean_proximity'].value_counts()`): '<1H OCEAN', 'INLAND', 'NEAR OCEAN', 'NEAR BAY', and 'ISLAND'.\n",
        "\n",
        "When `OneHotEncoder` is applied to this feature, it transforms each category into a new binary column. Therefore, **5 new columns** are created, one for each unique category. For each row, the column corresponding to its `ocean_proximity` category will have a value of 1, and the others will have 0."
      ]
